1. Live Detection — complete real-time object detection
Live Detection should continuously identify objects in the camera view while the user moves their phone or webcam around the environment.
Imagine the user points the camera towards a room containing a chair, table, laptop, bottle and person. OmniLens should identify each recognizable object, draw a separate bounding box around it, display its correct label and update the results as the camera moves.
How accurate should it be?
First, the object identity must be correct.
If the camera shows a laptop, the label should be “Laptop,” not “Television” or “Monitor” simply because they look similar. The system should identify only categories its model can reliably recognize. If it cannot distinguish two similar objects, it should not pretend that it can.
Second, the bounding boxes must be properly aligned.
Every box should enclose the correct visible object, as tightly as the detector reasonably allows. It must not float away from the object, cut off a large portion of it, or cover a different object. If two objects are close together, the system should keep their boxes separate whenever they are genuinely distinguishable.
This must remain correct when the camera preview is mirrored, the screen is resized, the user moves the camera, or the object moves within the frame.
Third, the confidence scores must be genuine.
If the interface shows 92%, that value must come from the actual model prediction and must not be invented. Also, model confidence is not necessarily the true probability that the prediction is correct.
Fourth, it must be exceptionally fast.
The user should see updated results with minimal delay. Detections should correspond to the latest relevant camera image, not an old frame from moments earlier. The camera view must remain responsive, even when multiple objects are visible.
What does 100% sensitivity mean?
Your slider must start at 100% every time. At maximum sensitivity, OmniLens should consider weaker-confidence object candidates instead of unnecessarily rejecting them.
This helps it find subtle background objects, smaller objects and partially visible objects. However, maximum sensitivity cannot guarantee that every object will be found. The system must still minimize false detections and duplicate boxes.
For example, a shadow should not automatically become a detected object just because the sensitivity is high.
What about objects that move or disappear?
When an object moves, its box should follow it correctly. When it leaves the camera view, its detection should disappear after the appropriate update. Old boxes must not remain floating on the screen.
If an object is temporarily obscured, OmniLens should not confidently claim that it can still see it when it cannot.
